00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:09.710
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:11.950
Welcome to this last
session of the day.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:16.480
Thank you for coming here and
taking up this time between you

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.680
and going to have some
fun over at AT&amp;T Park.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.870
What we're going to do today
is talk about Spotify's journey

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:26.320
from on premise data centers
to Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.640
I'm Peter-Mark Verwoerd.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.040
I'm a solutions
architect here at Google.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:33.040
Speaking with me today are
Max, who's a colleague of mine

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.360
from Google, and Ramon and
Josh, who are both from Spotify.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.190
And I will let each
of those gentlemen

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.920
introduce themselves when
they come up to speak.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.210
The four of us were all
part of a core team composed

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:50.050
of Googlers and Spotifiers that
made this migration a success.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.720
And so we're going to cover what
that was and how we did that.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.410
And to do that, I'll introduce
the next speaker, Max.

00:00:59.362 --> 00:01:04.282
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:04.282 --> 00:01:05.758
MAX CHARAS: That's nice.

00:01:05.758 --> 00:01:06.742
[INAUDIBLE]

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:14.000
So this presentation
is around how

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.640
we executed a multi-datacenter
migration to GCP.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.310
We worked with over 100
teams in two countries.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:28.130
And we migrated over 1,200
services and 20,000 data

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:28.895
processing jobs.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:35.420
This is a very active
collaboration between Google

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.740
and Spotify still.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.180
So the presentation is
split into four parts.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.940
We'll start with
an introduction,

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.010
where we cover the
Spotify organization

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:45.710
and kind of how it works.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:47.720
But we'll also walk
into the higher level

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.900
goals of the migration itself.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:52.610
After that, we have
two main chapters,

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:55.260
and they cover the migration.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.230
One is for the user
facing services

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:00.420
and the other is
for data processing.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:06.460
And after that, we have lessons
learned during the migration.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:10.050
So a note to you all, though,
this migration-- the strategy

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.930
is very tailored to
Spotify, both technically

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.250
and organizationally.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.630
So if you would want to do
something like this yourself,

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:19.510
it might look very different.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:23.378
So let's kick off the
intro and here is Ramon.

00:02:23.378 --> 00:02:28.757
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:28.757 --> 00:02:30.230
RAMON VAN ALTEREN: Hi.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:31.670
My name is Ramon van Alteren.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:34.170
I'm the Program Manager
for the entire cloud

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.590
migration at Spotify.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:41.000
Now before we start diving
into this migration, what

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.850
I would like to do is take you
through the two main reasons

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:46.230
why we wanted to move to cloud.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.140
And the first
reason is something

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:51.050
that applies at a
global company level.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:53.150
If you think about
the amount of effort

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:57.350
it takes to maintain compute
storage, and network capacity

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:00.920
for a global company
that serves more than 170

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.502
million users, that's a
sizable amount of work.

00:03:04.502 --> 00:03:05.960
Just think about
the amount of work

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:08.870
it just takes to replace the
servers that you depreciate

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:11.130
over every three years.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.230
That's a lot of work to do.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:16.900
And if I'm really honest, what
we really want to do at Spotify

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.790
is be the best music
service in the world.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:22.220
And none of that work working
on data centers actually

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.250
contributes directly to that.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:28.490
We'd much rather spend all that
focus, attention, and energy

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:30.620
into becoming the best
music service in the world

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:33.050
and build features for our
customers and our users

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:36.920
and our creators that make
it the best music service.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.770
So we saw a good
opportunity to take

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:41.600
some of that work
towards Google,

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.490
and allow our own
engineers to focus

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:46.220
on Spotify-specific actions.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.000
That's one thing.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.960
But the second thing is--

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.420
and that helps with
choosing a cloud,

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.210
is that if you start
analyzing this,

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.750
one of the things that
is really important to us

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:00.100
is fast product development.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.800
And if you start looking at
the ecosystem of services that

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.020
are slowly being
built on top of cloud

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:08.350
parameters, like instances,
networking, and storage

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:11.350
capacity, that's an
ecosystem of services

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:14.050
that allows you to
move much faster

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:17.890
and deliver a much better
product faster to users.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.390
Think about things like
BigQuery, or PubSub,

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:22.630
or Dataflow, those
things are building

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:24.680
blocks that allow us to
innovate a lot faster.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:33.050
So let's take a look
at where we started.

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:35.220
As Peter-Mark
already mentioned, we

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.650
had everything in
on-premise data centers.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.310
We're known for running a large
microservice architecture.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.290
At the time we started
this entire process,

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:46.370
we had about 1,200
different microservice

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:47.930
that served our users.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:51.230
By now, it's several
hundreds more.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:54.660
And we had a pretty
mature engineering culture

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:57.682
around that, with lots of
automation around deployments,

00:04:57.682 --> 00:04:59.640
spinning up instances,
spinning down instances.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.790
This was something people
could fairly easily do.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:05.040
We ran our service out
of four data center

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:06.810
sites in the world--

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:10.770
one on the West Coast, one on
the East Coast of US, and two

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.240
in Europe.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:17.240
Each of those sites is
essentially a standalone copy.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:19.460
Most of our backend
in those data centers

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:21.450
is essentially control
plane of the surface.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.312
So the content
delivery, the music

00:05:23.312 --> 00:05:25.520
that gets delivered to your
phone and to your desktop

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.249
or to your speaker comes out
of CDN networks and owned CDN.

00:05:29.249 --> 00:05:31.040
So it's not coming out
of our data centers.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:33.500
That's coming from
a separate place.

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:34.880
And we're big on data.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:36.860
We're a data-informed company.

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:39.680
We have about 20,000
data processing

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.860
jobs, 2,000 unique workflows,
and about 100 petabyte of data

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:44.660
when we started.

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:47.361
And all of that had
to move to cloud.

00:05:47.361 --> 00:05:50.000
And lastly, we had
about 100 teams.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.710
Important to know is probably
that those 100 teams each owned

00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:57.920
operational responsibility for
the services they developed.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:01.280
So that means they're
on call for those.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:03.942
But also own sort of the
entire business problem

00:06:03.942 --> 00:06:05.150
that they're trying to solve.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:11.490
Here's the three main goals
we had with this migration.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.920
First, link to the first reason.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.040
We wanted to minimize disruption
to our product development.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:18.660
But you can't stop
feature development

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:21.060
for the duration of
a cloud migration,

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.460
because that's completely
unacceptable both to our users

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:25.020
and our stakeholders.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:28.500
The second thing we felt was
really important was speed.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.980
Hybrid states-- where
you're in both states--

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:35.030
are both expensive, but
the rules are risky.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:37.130
You force engineering
teams to keep

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:41.040
two cognitive models, one for
on premise and one for cloud.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:42.540
And everything you
deploy, you need

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:44.820
to go through the process
of does this work on cloud,

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.260
does this work on premise--

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:49.440
for incident mode,
similar problems.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.990
The last goal we
had was clean up.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:54.270
This is a forcing function
for your long tail,

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:56.520
that you don't end up with
small things that are still

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:58.936
running in the data center,
and make sure that you finish.

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:05.150
What might come as a
big surprise to you

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:07.400
is that most of
this cloud strategy

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:09.600
actually came from the
infrastructure groups.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.480
The people maintaining
those data centers,

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:13.980
building the network,
building the instances,

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:15.590
ingesting all the
hardware-- those

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:19.720
were deeply involved in building
out this cloud strategy.

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:22.370
And we spend a lot of time
prepping and involving them

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:23.030
in that.

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:24.680
Part of that
involvement was talking

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:28.100
about what does your job look
like once we moved to cloud.

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:30.160
But it's to some
degree a scary thing

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:32.940
to move from a completely
owned stack to a cloud,

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:35.250
and what are you going to be
doing as an infrastructure

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:35.680
engineer?

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.096
And we spent a lot
of time talking

00:07:37.096 --> 00:07:38.740
about how that would evolve.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:41.592
And the net result of that
was that the entire group

00:07:41.592 --> 00:07:43.550
of engineers, which are
some of the most deeply

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:46.310
respected engineers inside
Spotify, have a lot of contact

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:49.610
with feature teams and often
own the larger scale core

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:53.090
services that we have, ended up
being advocates for our cloud

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:54.750
storage.

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:56.870
So we did a big
kick off with them.

00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:59.550
We built a whole
set of prototypes.

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:03.150
And during those prototypes,
we found two key blockers.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:05.850
One broker was the lack
of VPC when we started,

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:07.900
which is now there and
available for everyone.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:10.290
And the other problem
was related to VPN.

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:13.160
And we'll talk about-- in the
different migration tracks,

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:17.630
we'll talk about these
two different blockers.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:20.420
The last thing we did
was build a minimal use

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:22.560
case and a good one.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:25.550
If you want to move a
multimillion dollar company,

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:27.440
you can't show a
small prototype.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.900
You have to show you
actually can viably run

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:31.640
your business on top of cloud.

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:33.620
So we built a minimal
working prototype

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:36.440
that played music and
was capable of processing

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:38.782
the data coming out
to pay out royalties.

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:43.200
I think I would like
to spend a little bit

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.890
of a word on dependencies.

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:47.740
I think for every
migration, but especially

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:50.650
for cloud migrations,
dependencies are important.

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:52.150
But if you look
at a microservice

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:55.840
typically servicing a
single customer request,

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:59.370
takes about 10 to 15 services.

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:01.460
And the same is true
for our data processing.

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:03.520
A single data
processing workflow

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:07.720
will often involve multiple
intermediate datasets

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:10.560
and transformation
steps across these.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:13.210
Stop the world and everybody
migrate is not an option.

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:14.330
We talked about that.

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:16.050
So that means that
you need to find

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:18.750
a strategy that allows your
teams to move independently

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:21.390
without having to migrate
all their dependencies in one

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:21.902
go as well.

00:09:21.902 --> 00:09:23.360
And we felt it an
important problem

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:26.700
to solve first in
this migration.

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:29.070
And with that, I would
like to invite Peter-Mark

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:33.284
up here to talk about
some of these strategies.

00:09:33.284 --> 00:09:38.740
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:41.370
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: So
when we talk about migration

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:44.190
to the cloud in the abstract,
it breaks down generally

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:48.520
into about two
different strategies.

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:50.220
The first strategy
is lift and shift.

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:52.920
That's when you try to keep
your architecture as simple as

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:54.870
possible to whatever you have.

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:56.850
You minimize your changes
to virtual machines,

00:09:56.850 --> 00:10:01.740
application layer, databases,
network, and so on.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:05.430
The upside of this is that
you can focus on speed

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:08.640
and keep complexity down
while you're migrating.

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:10.440
The downside is that
you can potentially

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:12.960
increase your costs, because
your workloads can't really

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:17.190
use the elastic design patterns
or higher level services

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:19.440
available on Google Cloud.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:22.080
The other strategy is
some form of a rewrite.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.480
That's where you're taking
some part of your application,

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:27.570
or potentially all of it,
and completely rewriting it,

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:30.600
using more Cloud-native
products and architectures.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:34.620
Complexity of this is basically
directly proportional to how

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:37.140
complex is your
code base, how well

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.060
does the development
team know it,

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:41.250
and how much change
are you trying

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:44.930
to make to that application.

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:47.360
So as Max said before,
this presentation

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:49.680
is divided into two parts.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:52.010
And the first part will
cover the migration

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:55.010
of the user facing services,
what we're calling the services

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:56.120
migration.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:59.090
And the second
chapter will cover

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:02.900
the migration of data processing
workloads, so data migration.

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:08.420
Services migration used a lift
and shift strategy throughout.

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:10.610
And the data
migration use a hybrid

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:12.620
rewrite and lift
and shift approach,

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:15.260
depending on the kind of
workload that they had.

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:16.940
And so right now,
we're going to dive

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:19.765
into the services
migration with Ramon.

00:11:19.765 --> 00:11:20.715
Thank you.

00:11:20.715 --> 00:11:24.915
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:24.915 --> 00:11:26.290
RAMON VAN ALTEREN:
Hey, I'm back.

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:30.130
[LAUGHS] So we talked
about two blockers.

00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:32.470
So let's dive into
them a little bit.

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:33.600
The first one was VPC.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.590
There was no VPC
when we started this.

00:11:35.590 --> 00:11:36.820
It's a new feature.

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:39.976
We build it collaboratively with
Google, based on our feedback.

00:11:39.976 --> 00:11:41.350
But what we
essentially found was

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:45.270
that if you had
microservices in a project,

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:48.390
the only way for them to talk
to a microservice in Project B

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:51.330
was for the request to go
essentially out to the internet

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:52.935
and then come back in.

00:11:52.935 --> 00:11:54.810
And that didn't really
work that well for us.

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:57.600
We're a pretty latency
sensitive service.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.040
That was not acceptable.

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:03.870
We briefly try putting our
entire 120 team engineering

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:07.150
organization in one project, but
that really didn't work either.

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:09.660
So we quit that pretty quickly.

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:11.550
What we ended up-- or
what Google ended up

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:13.175
stepping up to the
plate and developing

00:12:13.175 --> 00:12:16.080
was VPC, which
allows you to build

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:17.790
similar to an
internal network that

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:19.350
connects multiple
of these projects

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:21.810
and that can cross-talk
easily enough.

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:23.970
So we ended up with
a decision where

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:25.890
we said, OK, every
service is going

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:28.140
to live in a single project.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:30.030
So not every team
has a single project,

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:32.260
but every service
has a single project.

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:35.472
And this gives team good
control over their own destiny.

00:12:35.472 --> 00:12:36.930
They get to do the
things that they

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:39.610
need to do for their
service in that project,

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:41.580
and if they shoot
themselves in the foot,

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:44.070
they shoot themselves in the
foot, not the entire company.

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:47.530
And that's important
to us as well.

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:51.940
I think the second blocker
that we found in this was VPN.

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:55.630
When we started, and
this is 2015-2016,

00:12:55.630 --> 00:13:01.300
right, we tried connecting our
data centers to Google Cloud

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:02.830
to solve the dependency problem.

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:04.300
So you can move
one service, and it

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:08.920
could connect to its
dependencies in data center.

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:10.990
That takes, with
1,200 microservice,

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:14.900
quite a lot of VPN bandwidth
and quality on that.

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:18.640
And to be honest, I think
the VPN service at that time

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:20.520
was more or less
scaled on, let's

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:22.950
connect an office with
25 developers in it.

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:25.350
And then we showed up with
four data centers and multiple

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:26.980
10,000s of service.

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:28.810
That didn't really
work that well.

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:30.730
Again, this was a
collaboration with Google

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:34.280
where the quality was
improved fairly rapidly.

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:36.470
And we managed to get
this working solidly.

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:39.220
So we build several
multiple gigabytes

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:41.350
of network pipes between
our different data

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:43.690
centers towards
Google Cloud to allow

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:46.910
for that dependency
problem to disappear.

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:49.370
Now after that, we
spent some time building

00:13:49.370 --> 00:13:51.500
just enough infrastructure
to make it actually

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:55.640
slightly easier to spin
up capacity in cloud

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:57.230
and it wasn't on premise--

00:13:57.230 --> 00:14:00.830
slightly faster, slightly
easier, same tool set.

00:14:04.130 --> 00:14:07.020
Another key realization
in the service migration

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:10.190
is that we could decouple
service migration

00:14:10.190 --> 00:14:12.860
from moving our user traffic.

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:16.380
170 million plus
users generate a lot

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:18.790
of traffic towards our service.

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:23.550
If you have to do that full bang
in one migration, that's hard.

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:26.890
You can do the work of
migrating a service to cloud,

00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:28.560
and do the development
work you need

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:31.920
for that without necessarily
moving all that traffic.

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:33.990
So we deliberately
separated these two

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:35.850
with different
roadmaps, where we

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:37.830
focused on getting
all these applications

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:41.622
ready to run in Google Cloud,
all these backend services.

00:14:41.622 --> 00:14:43.080
And then we had a
separate road map

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:45.690
that allowed us to gradually
connect more and more users

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:48.300
to GCP and that
allows us to control

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:53.220
both reliability, the user
experience, and our migration

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:53.880
speed.

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:56.520
And it allowed us to control
how much traffic was flowing

00:14:56.520 --> 00:14:57.870
over these VPN links, as well.

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:04.450
The next thing we did was
build a small migration team.

00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:07.550
It was a hybrid team staffed
out of both Spotify engineers

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:09.830
and Googlers.

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:11.990
And the first thing
we did as preparation

00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:13.940
for running that
migration team was we

00:15:13.940 --> 00:15:16.970
built a visualization of
the entire migration state

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:20.890
and I'll show that
to you in a second.

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:24.270
The second thing we did was we
built a completely standardized

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:25.860
migration sprint program.

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:28.360
How do you migrate a
backend service to cloud?

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:31.500
Well, here's a template
program on how to do that.

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:34.274
We built some teaching material
for common migration cases,

00:15:34.274 --> 00:15:35.940
like how do you migrate
your data store?

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:37.610
How do you make
migrate a Cassandra?

00:15:37.610 --> 00:15:40.200
How do you migrate
a Java application?

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:44.700
And we created a set of teaching
material about Google Cloud.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:45.720
What is changing?

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:47.272
What is important
for you to know?

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:52.800
Here's how that
visualization looked.

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:55.504
So every bubble is a system.

00:15:55.504 --> 00:15:57.670
The size of the bubble is
proportional to the amount

00:15:57.670 --> 00:16:00.730
of machines that the system
uses, the sort of capacity

00:16:00.730 --> 00:16:02.710
that the system has.

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:05.800
If it's red, it's
in a data center.

00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:10.190
If it's green, it's been
migrated to Google Cloud.

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:13.170
And this had a number of
interesting side effects.

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:16.760
I think one, it saved me as a
program manager a lot of time

00:16:16.760 --> 00:16:19.040
doing status updates about
the state of our migration,

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:20.623
because people could
just go and look.

00:16:20.623 --> 00:16:21.700
That was nice.

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:24.710
But the side effects were
interesting, I think.

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:26.930
What ended up happening was
that several people that

00:16:26.930 --> 00:16:29.630
would work in a team--

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:31.310
and if you look at
the visualization,

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:36.220
those blue-ish circles represent
teams and departments--

00:16:36.220 --> 00:16:39.200
so someone heading up one of
these departments or in a team,

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:40.880
would go and hunt for
these red bubbles.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:43.200
And go, look, why is
that not migrated?

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:45.750
And that was pretty
useful to us.

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:47.630
The other thing that
it did was that it

00:16:47.630 --> 00:16:49.880
created a real sense
of accomplishment

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:52.070
in teams that were migrating.

00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:54.934
From the start to the
finish of this migration,

00:16:54.934 --> 00:16:56.600
they could directly
see what impact they

00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:58.482
were having with their work.

00:16:58.482 --> 00:17:01.320
And we updated this
multiple times a day.

00:17:01.320 --> 00:17:03.470
So literally, once they
migrated the system,

00:17:03.470 --> 00:17:05.700
they could see the
bubble go green.

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:08.740
And that helped a
lot with motivation.

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:10.170
This is how it
looked by the time

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:13.980
we stopped doing the migration
with backend services in May

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:15.420
2017.

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:17.240
So we stopped the
sprint program.

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:20.397
Someone is wondering about the
big red bubble in the middle.

00:17:20.397 --> 00:17:21.480
That's our Hadoop cluster.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:23.017
We did not migrate that one.

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:29.870
So starting a sprint,
we did two things.

00:17:29.870 --> 00:17:32.500
One, and this is a really
important one, was focus.

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:34.660
What we essentially
did was we negotiated

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:36.920
with these different
product departments

00:17:36.920 --> 00:17:39.250
where there was room in
their product roadmap

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:41.560
to spend one or
two weeks migrating

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:45.140
so they would not get in
trouble with their deadlines.

00:17:45.140 --> 00:17:46.760
The con side of that
was that we asked

00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:49.310
them to stop all other work--

00:17:49.310 --> 00:17:53.120
so no feature work, no other
things, no minimal things,

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:55.490
no small things on
the side, nothing.

00:17:55.490 --> 00:17:58.820
All the engineers would focus,
for the duration of the sprint,

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:01.725
only on cloud migration.

00:18:01.725 --> 00:18:03.850
The second thing we did
was an intake meeting where

00:18:03.850 --> 00:18:05.099
we covered a number of things.

00:18:05.099 --> 00:18:08.024
One was what's the amount
of systems that you own?

00:18:08.024 --> 00:18:09.440
Which ones do you
want to migrate?

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:11.481
What are the ones that
you don't want to migrate?

00:18:11.481 --> 00:18:12.210
Why?

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:14.242
And that was a pretty
interesting start to do,

00:18:14.242 --> 00:18:15.700
because in many
cases, people said,

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:17.830
well, we're planning to
deprecate this anyway

00:18:17.830 --> 00:18:18.560
in three months.

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:20.640
So we can actually speed
it up and do it now.

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:23.890
So in many cases, it
actually reduced scope

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:25.720
instead of increased it.

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:28.670
The other thing we looked at
was large data storage systems.

00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:31.330
But if you own multiple
terabytes of data,

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:33.882
it actually takes
time to transfer that.

00:18:33.882 --> 00:18:36.340
And it's a bit silly to sit
with a whole group of engineers

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:38.381
to wait until it's
transferred to the other side.

00:18:38.381 --> 00:18:40.240
So often, if there were
very large systems,

00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:42.730
we would start that
transfer earlier

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:47.170
so we could hit the ground
running while starting sprint.

00:18:47.170 --> 00:18:49.810
The last thing we did was an
operational risk assessment.

00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:53.230
There is a difference between
moving a Tier 3 service that

00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:55.870
does a minimal feature
that degrades gracefully

00:18:55.870 --> 00:18:59.350
for our users and a Tier 1
service that if you fuck it up,

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:00.980
the entire service goes down.

00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:02.355
And then that
makes a difference,

00:19:02.355 --> 00:19:04.990
so it's important
to know about that.

00:19:04.990 --> 00:19:05.890
Excuse the language.

00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:08.972
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:08.972 --> 00:19:11.180
So with that, I would like
to ask the two people that

00:19:11.180 --> 00:19:15.740
joined me in the migration team
to come and talk about this.

00:19:15.740 --> 00:19:19.910
We split the work in
this in three big blocks.

00:19:19.910 --> 00:19:24.290
And about half of the work
was actual migration work.

00:19:24.290 --> 00:19:26.000
And the interesting
thing was that some

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:28.430
of the feedback we
got out of engineers

00:19:28.430 --> 00:19:32.390
was that it was boring.

00:19:32.390 --> 00:19:34.420
It actually worked
fairly simple.

00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:36.600
You just redeploy the
application on cloud

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:37.950
and it just works.

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:39.990
That was really nice to hear.

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:43.230
So it took far less time
than we thought it would,

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:46.240
in many cases.

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:49.050
What we did was we asked
people to focus on one region

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:50.620
they're taking
production traffic.

00:19:50.620 --> 00:19:53.430
But also spin minimal capacity
up in the other regions

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:55.960
that we were using.

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:59.250
And then that one region
was taking enough traffic

00:19:59.250 --> 00:20:03.590
to see any performance
or reliability issues.

00:20:03.590 --> 00:20:05.850
A key thing was, where
you get these people want

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:08.410
to fiddle with the systems and
make them better at that time.

00:20:08.410 --> 00:20:09.986
So we were pretty
strict about no,

00:20:09.986 --> 00:20:11.652
we're not cleaning
up tech [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:11.652 --> 00:20:14.167
We're migrating at this time.

00:20:14.167 --> 00:20:16.500
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: So once
the majority of the systems

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:20.100
had been migrated, usually
by the end of the first week,

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:24.810
we would deliberately, in
a controlled, recorded way,

00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:27.450
actually induce failures in
their newly migrated systems

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:30.210
to see how comfortable
and where the teams were

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:32.615
in managing incidents.

00:20:32.615 --> 00:20:34.740
I mean, basically, we just
started breaking things,

00:20:34.740 --> 00:20:37.110
slowly, until they
started noticing.

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:39.210
And then we watched
and saw and recorded

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:41.490
how they would recover.

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:46.320
So while it was absolutely
fun to break things

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:48.750
and watch teams
scramble, there were

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:52.080
a couple of interesting
things that came from that.

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:54.467
The first thing-- there were
a couple of obvious ones.

00:20:54.467 --> 00:20:56.550
So the first one was make
sure that the monitoring

00:20:56.550 --> 00:21:01.120
systems were properly extended
to the new cloud deployment.

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:03.330
Make sure that
that's all working.

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:05.970
If we started breaking something
in and the team didn't notice,

00:21:05.970 --> 00:21:07.636
that was obviously a
big red flag that's

00:21:07.636 --> 00:21:09.190
something they need to monitor.

00:21:09.190 --> 00:21:12.960
But I think what was really
interesting was when something

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:15.320
broke, team noticed,
recovered, and we're

00:21:15.320 --> 00:21:17.880
debriefing and talking
about how we did what we did

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:20.280
or what the failure mode was.

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:23.334
And teams starting to look into
different ways of monitoring,

00:21:23.334 --> 00:21:25.000
and said, well, you
know, we noticed it.

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:27.240
But if we were monitoring
on this metric instead

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:28.980
of that one, not
only would we have

00:21:28.980 --> 00:21:30.960
noticed, but we'd have
gotten to the root cause

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:31.750
much more quickly.

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:33.660
And so that kind of
iterative process

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:36.000
over deliberately breaking
things really kind of

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:37.510
helped a lot of teams.

00:21:37.510 --> 00:21:40.692
And then finally,
we had this playbook

00:21:40.692 --> 00:21:42.650
that they get start going
to and start building

00:21:42.650 --> 00:21:44.540
on for failure
modes in the cloud

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:47.157
that they may not have
had to deal with before

00:21:47.157 --> 00:21:47.990
or encounter before.

00:21:47.990 --> 00:21:50.840
And so that was really a great
experience to kind of watch

00:21:50.840 --> 00:21:54.740
that discovery process and
kind of build on that as we

00:21:54.740 --> 00:21:56.133
went throughout the migration.

00:21:59.090 --> 00:21:59.840
MAX CHARAS: Great.

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:03.590
So we also had four
educational packages.

00:22:03.590 --> 00:22:05.622
And you can see these
at the bottom here.

00:22:05.622 --> 00:22:07.330
I think it's important
to state, like, we

00:22:07.330 --> 00:22:10.660
did this for more or less
every team that we encountered.

00:22:10.660 --> 00:22:13.150
So they had to go through
these lectures and workshops

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:15.260
where we discussed
different topics.

00:22:15.260 --> 00:22:17.170
The first was GCP support.

00:22:17.170 --> 00:22:20.800
And that was really about
how to interact with support.

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:23.290
And it was about interacting
with support, both

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:27.340
during incidents as well as
for more long term development

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:28.570
support.

00:22:28.570 --> 00:22:31.090
And it was really important,
the small things here,

00:22:31.090 --> 00:22:33.700
like seeing that people actually
created support accounts

00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:36.300
and opened the case
to see how it worked.

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:39.827
So when in the future they
would have an incident,

00:22:39.827 --> 00:22:42.160
everybody in the team would
feel comfortable interacting

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:43.930
with support.

00:22:43.930 --> 00:22:46.360
The second one was
networking and what

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:49.510
we covered here was really
the different semantics

00:22:49.510 --> 00:22:52.480
of networking between
GCP and on prem.

00:22:52.480 --> 00:22:54.890
Because the teams were
used to operating on prem

00:22:54.890 --> 00:22:58.450
in very specific data centers.

00:22:58.450 --> 00:23:00.190
The third was a
product overview,

00:23:00.190 --> 00:23:02.050
where we went
through, most notably,

00:23:02.050 --> 00:23:04.930
the different database
options within GCP.

00:23:04.930 --> 00:23:07.720
And the goal here was to
allow the different teams

00:23:07.720 --> 00:23:10.570
to feel comfortable moving
to more managed solutions

00:23:10.570 --> 00:23:11.397
in the future.

00:23:11.397 --> 00:23:13.480
Now we didn't do this
during the migration itself.

00:23:13.480 --> 00:23:17.080
This was something
for future purposes.

00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:20.140
And last, but not least,
we also covered costs,

00:23:20.140 --> 00:23:23.200
so that everybody in the
team would feel comfortable

00:23:23.200 --> 00:23:26.780
monitoring and understanding
the different semantics of costs

00:23:26.780 --> 00:23:27.430
on GCP.

00:23:32.729 --> 00:23:35.020
RAMON VAN ALTEREN: So as I
showed in the visualization,

00:23:35.020 --> 00:23:38.210
by May, we had done
the migration sprint

00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:41.860
with nearly every
team in the company.

00:23:41.860 --> 00:23:45.100
And at that point, even during
those migrations sprints,

00:23:45.100 --> 00:23:48.420
we slowly started increasing
traffic towards Google Cloud.

00:23:48.420 --> 00:23:50.450
Well, we can control
where users connect.

00:23:50.450 --> 00:23:55.570
So more and more users
started connecting to GCP.

00:23:55.570 --> 00:23:57.490
And during the
migration, started

00:23:57.490 --> 00:23:58.960
gradually increasing that.

00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:02.240
It allows us to measure and
control the user experience.

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:04.060
This was a key thing
for us to make sure

00:24:04.060 --> 00:24:06.310
that it continued to be good.

00:24:06.310 --> 00:24:09.900
We have a reputation for
carrying around all the world's

00:24:09.900 --> 00:24:12.400
music in your pocket, and if
you press play, it starts.

00:24:12.400 --> 00:24:14.660
So this user quality
was important for us.

00:24:14.660 --> 00:24:16.780
And we wanted to make
sure that that did not

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:18.970
degrade for moving to cloud.

00:24:18.970 --> 00:24:22.780
And this allowed us to measure
that really effectively.

00:24:22.780 --> 00:24:25.180
The other thing we did was
manage reliability risks

00:24:25.180 --> 00:24:26.200
with this.

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:28.960
And the last thing is
we were able to control

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:32.050
the amount of users we put on
cloud, based on the migration

00:24:32.050 --> 00:24:32.980
speed that we had.

00:24:32.980 --> 00:24:35.590
The more services we had
in GCP, the more users

00:24:35.590 --> 00:24:37.390
we managed to direct there.

00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:41.320
And then, by the time we had
all applications or all services

00:24:41.320 --> 00:24:44.230
on GCP, we did a
number of things

00:24:44.230 --> 00:24:48.530
while increasing more and
more traffic towards 100%.

00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:51.640
The reliability program we
ran with individual teams

00:24:51.640 --> 00:24:54.824
helps teams reason about their
reliability for their system.

00:24:54.824 --> 00:24:56.740
There is also an aspect
of the entire service.

00:24:56.740 --> 00:25:00.640
So one of the things we did is
we ran company-wide reliability

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:03.320
exercises, where we closed
an entire data center, where

00:25:03.320 --> 00:25:05.169
we closed an entire
cloud region,

00:25:05.169 --> 00:25:07.210
and made sure that we
could still provide service

00:25:07.210 --> 00:25:09.900
to our users.

00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:14.190
Just before Christmas in
December of last year, 2017,

00:25:14.190 --> 00:25:16.510
we hit 100% users.

00:25:16.510 --> 00:25:19.830
But at that point, the first
data center was already closed.

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:23.220
We closed our San Jose data
center here on the West Coast

00:25:23.220 --> 00:25:26.390
in October 2017.

00:25:26.390 --> 00:25:27.800
And that's a pretty
strong signal

00:25:27.800 --> 00:25:29.570
for people with long
tail applications

00:25:29.570 --> 00:25:31.760
that are still running
in data centers--

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:34.780
that they need to get a move on.

00:25:34.780 --> 00:25:36.410
There's a roadmap for the rest.

00:25:36.410 --> 00:25:38.410
We have already closed
our secondary data center

00:25:38.410 --> 00:25:40.570
in the US.

00:25:40.570 --> 00:25:41.950
And there's two
data centers left

00:25:41.950 --> 00:25:44.290
in Europe, which were already
scaled down significantly.

00:25:44.290 --> 00:25:48.620
But they'll close by
the end of this year.

00:25:48.620 --> 00:25:51.020
This concludes our overview
of the services migration.

00:25:51.020 --> 00:25:53.810
I would now like to hand over
to Josh, my colleague to talk

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:55.644
about the data migration.

00:25:55.644 --> 00:26:02.272
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:02.272 --> 00:26:03.480
JOSH BAER: Thanks, everybody.

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:05.760
So my name is Josh Baer.

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:07.440
I lead the data migration.

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:09.940
And that's what I'm going
to talk about right now.

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:12.360
So just to recap a
little bit about where

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:16.290
we were before we moved
our stuff over to GCP--

00:26:16.290 --> 00:26:19.404
we called ourselves the largest
Hadoop cluster in Europe.

00:26:19.404 --> 00:26:20.820
And we were able
to call ourselves

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:23.880
that because nobody ever
corrected us, so it worked.

00:26:23.880 --> 00:26:26.760
We were certainly one of the
largest clusters, for sure.

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:31.080
So we had about 20,000 jobs that
ran every day from around 2000

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:32.370
workloads.

00:26:32.370 --> 00:26:34.420
And as Ramon mentioned
at the beginning,

00:26:34.420 --> 00:26:38.400
this was from as many as 100
different teams distributed

00:26:38.400 --> 00:26:42.560
throughout Europe and the US.

00:26:42.560 --> 00:26:46.490
So this is a view of what
our dependency graph looks

00:26:46.490 --> 00:26:49.250
like, all those jobs
interacting with each other.

00:26:49.250 --> 00:26:51.980
And this is actually
a vast simplification

00:26:51.980 --> 00:26:53.060
of our dependency tree.

00:26:53.060 --> 00:26:54.950
Because if we
showed the full one,

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:58.070
it would be way too wide and
way too deep to actually project

00:26:58.070 --> 00:27:01.550
it, and you wouldn't
actually see a lot of it.

00:27:01.550 --> 00:27:03.170
So the crazy thing
about this graphic

00:27:03.170 --> 00:27:06.260
is each little smudge that
you might see there is a job.

00:27:06.260 --> 00:27:09.650
And each job has an input
and each job has an output.

00:27:09.650 --> 00:27:11.540
And those inputs are
the dependencies.

00:27:11.540 --> 00:27:15.680
And those dependencies could be
from a totally different team

00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:17.220
than the team that you work on.

00:27:17.220 --> 00:27:18.920
And it could be in
a different location

00:27:18.920 --> 00:27:21.170
than the location you work on.

00:27:21.170 --> 00:27:24.980
So it's fairly complex to try
to move these, all those jobs,

00:27:24.980 --> 00:27:29.754
over to GCP without causing
downstream failures.

00:27:29.754 --> 00:27:31.170
And that was really
the challenge.

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:33.805
And overcoming this challenge
through our strategy

00:27:33.805 --> 00:27:35.554
is what I'm going to
talk about right now.

00:27:37.530 --> 00:27:39.300
So you can now
understand the complexity

00:27:39.300 --> 00:27:41.490
of what we had to move.

00:27:41.490 --> 00:27:43.260
And one thing we
could have done is

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:45.510
to tell everybody to stop
everything they're doing,

00:27:45.510 --> 00:27:48.990
shut down the Hadoop cluster,
copy all the data over to GCP,

00:27:48.990 --> 00:27:52.045
copy the state, and start
things back up again.

00:27:52.045 --> 00:27:53.670
Now unfortunately,
we couldn't do this.

00:27:53.670 --> 00:27:55.410
Because as you've
seen in this graphics,

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:58.770
even with 160 gigabit
per second network

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:00.714
link that we had to
Google initially,

00:28:00.714 --> 00:28:02.130
it would take us
around two months

00:28:02.130 --> 00:28:05.660
to copy all the data that
we had on the cluster.

00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:08.610
And because we're a business
that relies on our data

00:28:08.610 --> 00:28:11.190
to pay our labels, and
we rely on our labels

00:28:11.190 --> 00:28:14.220
to provide content, and
we rely on our content

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:16.230
to provide a service
for our users,

00:28:16.230 --> 00:28:17.820
we wouldn't be
much of a business

00:28:17.820 --> 00:28:21.510
if we were down for two months.

00:28:21.510 --> 00:28:26.490
And since every node
in the dependency tree

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:28.260
earlier could represent
individual team

00:28:28.260 --> 00:28:29.850
we had a rough job
of moving things

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:32.350
without disrupting other users.

00:28:32.350 --> 00:28:34.080
So what could we do?

00:28:34.080 --> 00:28:36.360
So our strategy was
to copy lots of data,

00:28:36.360 --> 00:28:41.320
essentially to duplicate data,
both on premise and on GCP.

00:28:41.320 --> 00:28:44.190
So as you moved your pipeline,
as you moved your job over

00:28:44.190 --> 00:28:48.420
to GCP, what you would do is you
would copy your dependencies,

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:49.260
all the data.

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:51.010
You would set up
a copy job to copy

00:28:51.010 --> 00:28:53.040
you dependencies over
to GCP, and then you

00:28:53.040 --> 00:28:55.200
could port your job over.

00:28:55.200 --> 00:28:57.240
Then if you had
downstream consumers,

00:28:57.240 --> 00:28:59.370
you might have to copy
the output of your job

00:28:59.370 --> 00:29:01.740
back to our
on-premise cluster, so

00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:04.970
that the downstream
consumers weren't broken.

00:29:04.970 --> 00:29:07.580
And since the bulk of our
data migration lasted around 6

00:29:07.580 --> 00:29:11.720
to 12 months, we were
running a lot of these jobs

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:13.700
to fill gaps in our
dependency trees.

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:15.890
As you can see in the
graphics here, at peak,

00:29:15.890 --> 00:29:18.770
we had around 80,000
jobs that copied data

00:29:18.770 --> 00:29:21.950
from on premise to the
cloud and around 30,000

00:29:21.950 --> 00:29:26.270
that copied data back to on
premise, so a lot of copies,

00:29:26.270 --> 00:29:30.570
a lot of duplicate data,
and a lot of complexity.

00:29:30.570 --> 00:29:33.500
And I mention that these
copy jobs were not exactly

00:29:33.500 --> 00:29:35.990
easy things to get right.

00:29:35.990 --> 00:29:40.130
So especially early on,
we had the 160 gigabit

00:29:40.130 --> 00:29:42.120
per second network bandwidth.

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:45.784
And we found problems
that when jobs were broken

00:29:45.784 --> 00:29:48.200
and they had to catch up, they
would saturate that network

00:29:48.200 --> 00:29:48.980
bandwidth.

00:29:48.980 --> 00:29:50.570
And then we had our
high priority jobs

00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:52.190
that were getting blocked
by maybe jobs that

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:53.280
aren't such high priority.

00:29:53.280 --> 00:29:55.340
So we had to figure out
how to get that working.

00:29:55.340 --> 00:29:57.620
That was one challenge that
we disrupted over time.

00:29:57.620 --> 00:30:01.236
And we learned it's massively
important in this transition,

00:30:01.236 --> 00:30:03.860
when you're in the hybrid state,
to over provision your network

00:30:03.860 --> 00:30:05.690
bandwidth.

00:30:05.690 --> 00:30:07.754
Another problem
that we ran into,

00:30:07.754 --> 00:30:09.170
or another lesson
that we learned,

00:30:09.170 --> 00:30:12.440
was to avoid using the VPN
whenever possible in these copy

00:30:12.440 --> 00:30:13.260
jobs.

00:30:13.260 --> 00:30:16.640
And fortunately, GCS has
application layer security,

00:30:16.640 --> 00:30:19.100
so you really don't need
to use the VPN to copy data

00:30:19.100 --> 00:30:22.070
back and forth.

00:30:22.070 --> 00:30:24.430
So while we still
couldn't tell everybody

00:30:24.430 --> 00:30:28.230
to stop everything they're
doing and move everything

00:30:28.230 --> 00:30:29.740
to the cluster,
what we could do is

00:30:29.740 --> 00:30:32.290
say, stop everything
new that you're

00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:35.140
doing on our on-premise
cluster and start writing

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:38.014
all the new things on GCP.

00:30:38.014 --> 00:30:39.430
And the benefit
of that is there's

00:30:39.430 --> 00:30:42.610
some natural attrition as people
are rewriting or replacing

00:30:42.610 --> 00:30:43.570
their jobs.

00:30:43.570 --> 00:30:47.320
They'll just kill off their
old ones on the on-premise.

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:49.690
But what about the jobs
that already existed

00:30:49.690 --> 00:30:52.300
and weren't being replaced?

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:55.445
So they had two paths to choose
from, as was mentioned earlier,

00:30:55.445 --> 00:30:57.070
and that's what I'm
going to highlight.

00:30:57.070 --> 00:31:01.240
The first path was called
the forklifting path.

00:31:01.240 --> 00:31:05.300
So pure lift and shift
wouldn't work for us.

00:31:05.300 --> 00:31:07.070
And there's a few
reasons for that.

00:31:07.070 --> 00:31:08.710
The first one is
that we really wanted

00:31:08.710 --> 00:31:12.460
to move away from this process
where we had one single Hadoop

00:31:12.460 --> 00:31:14.770
cluster that managed
all our data jobs

00:31:14.770 --> 00:31:17.230
and one team that managed
that Hadoop cluster that

00:31:17.230 --> 00:31:19.900
bore all the operational
responsibility if it

00:31:19.900 --> 00:31:20.650
was up or down.

00:31:26.170 --> 00:31:28.060
We also had to adapt
to the assumptions

00:31:28.060 --> 00:31:29.890
that we made from
on-premise cluster

00:31:29.890 --> 00:31:32.290
to what the reality was in GCP.

00:31:32.290 --> 00:31:36.280
So for example, our
orchestration framework really

00:31:36.280 --> 00:31:38.710
depended on atomic moves.

00:31:38.710 --> 00:31:42.250
And in GCS, they don't
have that same atomicity,

00:31:42.250 --> 00:31:46.219
so we had to build some
tooling to bridge that gap.

00:31:46.219 --> 00:31:48.760
So at the start of migration,
we actually spend a lot of time

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:50.950
to build infrastructure
and tooling to make

00:31:50.950 --> 00:31:53.530
it easier for our teams to use.

00:31:53.530 --> 00:31:55.570
And I want to highlight
a few of those tools

00:31:55.570 --> 00:31:58.380
that we built in our process.

00:31:58.380 --> 00:32:00.730
And I should mention that
all of these are open source,

00:32:00.730 --> 00:32:02.890
so you can check
them out, and maybe

00:32:02.890 --> 00:32:04.570
they will be useful to you.

00:32:04.570 --> 00:32:07.840
The first tool that I
want to mention is Styx.

00:32:07.840 --> 00:32:10.480
So early in our previous
on-premise world,

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:12.700
we had the concept of
these long-lived edge

00:32:12.700 --> 00:32:14.680
nodes that launched jobs.

00:32:14.680 --> 00:32:17.650
We wanted to move to a
world where you could just

00:32:17.650 --> 00:32:20.830
containerize your
job in Docker and use

00:32:20.830 --> 00:32:22.272
Kubernetes to schedule it.

00:32:22.272 --> 00:32:23.980
But we had to build
a scheduler for that,

00:32:23.980 --> 00:32:26.470
and that's what Styx is.

00:32:26.470 --> 00:32:29.110
So in addition to being more
fault tolerant, this process,

00:32:29.110 --> 00:32:31.960
it allows us to be very
flexible across the cloud

00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:34.540
and very flexible with
how we use our resources.

00:32:34.540 --> 00:32:40.310
And we don't have these really
hand-tuned edge nodes anymore.

00:32:40.310 --> 00:32:42.160
The second tool that
I want to highlight

00:32:42.160 --> 00:32:44.170
is something called Spydra.

00:32:44.170 --> 00:32:49.070
And in a few words, Spydra is
Hadoop cluster as a service.

00:32:49.070 --> 00:32:50.660
Now you might be
thinking, there's

00:32:50.660 --> 00:32:53.450
already Hadoop cluster as
a service that GCP offers.

00:32:53.450 --> 00:32:55.520
Its Dataproc, right?

00:32:55.520 --> 00:32:56.140
That's true.

00:32:56.140 --> 00:32:58.940
But what we wanted to do is
go one level of abstraction

00:32:58.940 --> 00:33:00.994
higher so that our
users really didn't

00:33:00.994 --> 00:33:02.660
have to worry about
learning a new tool,

00:33:02.660 --> 00:33:04.640
learning how Dataproc offers.

00:33:04.640 --> 00:33:06.350
They just write
their job, and we'd

00:33:06.350 --> 00:33:08.930
take care of the spinning up
and the tearing down of clusters

00:33:08.930 --> 00:33:09.837
by ourselves.

00:33:09.837 --> 00:33:11.420
And we'd take care
of the net tooling,

00:33:11.420 --> 00:33:15.420
centralizing the logging,
so that if their job fails,

00:33:15.420 --> 00:33:18.290
they can still figure
out what's wrong with it.

00:33:18.290 --> 00:33:21.110
So this was great and
troubleshooting was still easy.

00:33:21.110 --> 00:33:24.560
And there was no need for that
centralized infrastructure team

00:33:24.560 --> 00:33:31.000
and no need for that centralized
big main Hadoop cluster.

00:33:31.000 --> 00:33:33.750
So the forklifting path
was primarily the path

00:33:33.750 --> 00:33:36.510
that teams had to take if they
had a lot of other priorities

00:33:36.510 --> 00:33:40.835
and they couldn't take
the time to rewrite.

00:33:40.835 --> 00:33:42.960
But I'm going to talk about
the rewriting path now.

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:46.600
And that was really advantageous
for some other teams.

00:33:46.600 --> 00:33:49.170
So in this path, you would
either rewrite your job

00:33:49.170 --> 00:33:52.020
using BigQuery or
a tool called Scio,

00:33:52.020 --> 00:33:53.700
which I'll talk about
in a second, which

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:56.300
is essentially Dataflow.

00:33:56.300 --> 00:33:59.220
So the benefits of this is this
was massively exciting for all

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:00.350
our engineers, right?

00:34:00.350 --> 00:34:03.230
One of the real advantages
for moving to GCP

00:34:03.230 --> 00:34:05.117
is our engineers
could go and play

00:34:05.117 --> 00:34:06.950
with the latest and
greatest tech, the stuff

00:34:06.950 --> 00:34:09.770
that we had read about
in papers, that we always

00:34:09.770 --> 00:34:11.730
had wanted to try out.

00:34:11.730 --> 00:34:14.070
So this was exciting for them.

00:34:14.070 --> 00:34:16.120
And the benefit for
the teams is this path

00:34:16.120 --> 00:34:17.750
is actually fully managed, too.

00:34:17.750 --> 00:34:21.199
So they don't even have to worry
about spinning up and tearing

00:34:21.199 --> 00:34:23.300
down these Hadoop clusters.

00:34:23.300 --> 00:34:26.659
We just can outsource
that to the Google teams

00:34:26.659 --> 00:34:30.159
to provide the managed
infrastructure for us.

00:34:30.159 --> 00:34:32.389
And this was also useful
for teams that didn't really

00:34:32.389 --> 00:34:36.170
feel comfortable just porting
over their jobs using the lift

00:34:36.170 --> 00:34:38.672
and shift, using the
forklifting path.

00:34:38.672 --> 00:34:40.130
And they might not
feel comfortable

00:34:40.130 --> 00:34:43.388
because maybe they inherited
these data jobs somewhere

00:34:43.388 --> 00:34:45.679
in the past and they never
actually had looked at them,

00:34:45.679 --> 00:34:47.553
so they wanted to dig
into them a little bit.

00:34:47.553 --> 00:34:49.670
And while they were
digging into them,

00:34:49.670 --> 00:34:52.560
they might as well rewrite them.

00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:55.489
So like the forklifting
path, we ended up

00:34:55.489 --> 00:34:58.310
creating some tooling to
support our users in this.

00:34:58.310 --> 00:35:00.314
And the main tool that
I'd like to support,

00:35:00.314 --> 00:35:02.480
that's also open source--
you should go check it out

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:04.250
in GitHub--

00:35:04.250 --> 00:35:06.560
is a tool called Scio.

00:35:06.560 --> 00:35:09.770
So Scio is essentially
a library that's

00:35:09.770 --> 00:35:12.670
wrapped on top of
Beam or Dataflow,

00:35:12.670 --> 00:35:15.320
which simplified
some of the less

00:35:15.320 --> 00:35:19.690
familiar functions of Dataflow
for our data engineers.

00:35:19.690 --> 00:35:22.100
And it was a bit closer
to scalding, something

00:35:22.100 --> 00:35:27.340
that we used pretty extensively
in our Hadoop world.

00:35:27.340 --> 00:35:29.430
And the great
additional benefit--

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:32.580
it's got real tight
integration with BigQuery.

00:35:32.580 --> 00:35:34.950
So if you just want a
few specific columns

00:35:34.950 --> 00:35:36.480
that you want to
process over, you

00:35:36.480 --> 00:35:38.190
can use BigQuery to
get those columns,

00:35:38.190 --> 00:35:40.720
and then use Scio
to process them.

00:35:40.720 --> 00:35:42.930
So it's been really
successful, and I'd highly

00:35:42.930 --> 00:35:45.420
recommend you check it out.

00:35:45.420 --> 00:35:49.830
So but this path, too, wasn't
without its challenges.

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:53.970
The biggest thing is it required
a much larger time investment

00:35:53.970 --> 00:35:54.782
from teams, right?

00:35:54.782 --> 00:35:56.490
They're not just
porting their jobs over,

00:35:56.490 --> 00:35:58.320
they're actually rewriting it.

00:35:58.320 --> 00:36:01.500
And as engineers,
what usually happened

00:36:01.500 --> 00:36:04.286
is when you start to
rewrite it, you say, hm.

00:36:04.286 --> 00:36:06.660
That thing I did-- what was
I thinking two years ago when

00:36:06.660 --> 00:36:07.201
I wrote that?

00:36:07.201 --> 00:36:09.180
I would want to
re-architect it, too.

00:36:09.180 --> 00:36:11.820
So when teams usually
told me that they

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:13.840
would rewrite their jobs
in around two weeks,

00:36:13.840 --> 00:36:15.300
it usually lasted a month.

00:36:15.300 --> 00:36:17.074
And when teams told
me, it was a month.

00:36:17.074 --> 00:36:18.240
It was usually three months.

00:36:18.240 --> 00:36:20.955
So that leads into this quote
here, Hofstadter's Law--

00:36:20.955 --> 00:36:24.990
that it always took longer, even
taking into account that law.

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:27.010
So towards the middle
and end of our migration,

00:36:27.010 --> 00:36:31.020
we actually had to tell people
to avoid the rewrite path

00:36:31.020 --> 00:36:32.040
altogether.

00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:34.800
Migrate their stuff using
the forklifting path, then

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:38.550
if they really did want to
rewrite it, or re-architect it,

00:36:38.550 --> 00:36:40.957
it was all already on GCP.

00:36:40.957 --> 00:36:42.790
So we could still hit
our migration targets.

00:36:45.970 --> 00:36:47.170
So that's our strategy.

00:36:47.170 --> 00:36:50.110
I want to quickly highlight
the tactics that we

00:36:50.110 --> 00:36:53.660
used to move teams over to.

00:36:53.660 --> 00:36:58.520
So at this point, we've provided
the tooling for the teams

00:36:58.520 --> 00:37:00.117
to forklift their jobs over.

00:37:00.117 --> 00:37:01.700
And we've provided
libraries that they

00:37:01.700 --> 00:37:05.190
could use to rewrite their
jobs on GCP natively.

00:37:05.190 --> 00:37:07.300
But for other teams,
the reality was still

00:37:07.300 --> 00:37:09.350
that ramping up to speed
with these new tools

00:37:09.350 --> 00:37:10.042
was a challenge.

00:37:10.042 --> 00:37:12.500
There were new things, and they
didn't really have capacity

00:37:12.500 --> 00:37:16.430
to learn new things, or maybe
they had specific edge cases

00:37:16.430 --> 00:37:18.690
that the tools didn't cover.

00:37:18.690 --> 00:37:22.850
So to solve that, we decided
to create a temporary team.

00:37:22.850 --> 00:37:25.490
Temporary teams can be
composed of both infrastructure

00:37:25.490 --> 00:37:28.880
engineers and the feature
teams, the people that

00:37:28.880 --> 00:37:31.190
understood the business logic.

00:37:31.190 --> 00:37:33.440
So the benefit of having
our infrastructure engineers

00:37:33.440 --> 00:37:35.523
is that they're the people
that created the tools.

00:37:35.523 --> 00:37:37.160
So they understand
really well how

00:37:37.160 --> 00:37:41.390
to adapt jobs to get
them to run on GCP.

00:37:41.390 --> 00:37:44.750
And the benefit of having
the feature teams work on it

00:37:44.750 --> 00:37:46.424
is they understood
the business logic.

00:37:46.424 --> 00:37:47.840
And they were
especially important

00:37:47.840 --> 00:37:51.175
because they would own
the jobs when it's on GCP.

00:37:51.175 --> 00:37:52.550
And they would
have to understand

00:37:52.550 --> 00:37:55.280
how their logic worked.

00:37:55.280 --> 00:37:57.770
So this task force
lasted around one

00:37:57.770 --> 00:38:02.810
to two weeks of highly focused
sprints with one main goal.

00:38:02.810 --> 00:38:06.660
And that was to take all your
jobs, port them over to GCP,

00:38:06.660 --> 00:38:08.970
and oh, yeah, don't break
your downstream consumers.

00:38:08.970 --> 00:38:12.030
Use those copy jobs.

00:38:12.030 --> 00:38:14.637
So now that was the
tactics and strategy.

00:38:14.637 --> 00:38:16.970
I'm going to quickly highlight
what our data stack looks

00:38:16.970 --> 00:38:19.770
like now on top of GCP.

00:38:19.770 --> 00:38:22.460
So the first tool that I'd
like to highlight is BigQuery.

00:38:22.460 --> 00:38:23.979
And maybe some of you saw--

00:38:23.979 --> 00:38:25.520
Nicole was in the
audience over there

00:38:25.520 --> 00:38:28.835
to do a talk yesterday on how
we use BigQuery at Spotify.

00:38:31.250 --> 00:38:33.080
When we started
seeing BigQuery used,

00:38:33.080 --> 00:38:36.500
it really to underscores
a powerful explosion

00:38:36.500 --> 00:38:39.500
in lowering the friction for
asking and answering questions

00:38:39.500 --> 00:38:41.120
and how adoption
really increased.

00:38:41.120 --> 00:38:45.910
As you see now, we have around
25% of Spotify's employees use

00:38:45.910 --> 00:38:46.615
BigQuery.

00:38:46.615 --> 00:38:47.990
And before the
migration, I think

00:38:47.990 --> 00:38:50.750
we had around 50 monthly
active users for Hive

00:38:50.750 --> 00:38:54.770
that were asking and answering
jobs using that [INAUDIBLE]..

00:38:54.770 --> 00:38:57.610
Another tool that I would
like to highlight is Pub/Sub.

00:38:57.610 --> 00:39:01.610
So Pub/Sub feeds both our
batch and our real time data

00:39:01.610 --> 00:39:03.975
processing jobs.

00:39:03.975 --> 00:39:05.350
And the great
thing about Pub/Sub

00:39:05.350 --> 00:39:09.000
is it's significantly
lowered our operational costs

00:39:09.000 --> 00:39:11.910
from before the migration.

00:39:11.910 --> 00:39:15.660
Then I'd like to highlight
both Dataflow and Dataproc.

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:18.510
So we're heavily,
heavy users of both.

00:39:18.510 --> 00:39:22.230
Dataflow, in particular,
I think a few months ago I

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:23.070
talked to the PM.

00:39:23.070 --> 00:39:25.434
And we were the
largest Dataflow user.

00:39:25.434 --> 00:39:27.600
I think we're still at least
one of the largest now.

00:39:27.600 --> 00:39:30.437
We run around for
5,000 jobs per day.

00:39:30.437 --> 00:39:32.020
And almost all of
these jobs are using

00:39:32.020 --> 00:39:38.380
Scio, which our engineers
absolutely love to use.

00:39:38.380 --> 00:39:42.304
So you might be thinking, what
happened to Europe's largest

00:39:42.304 --> 00:39:43.220
Hadoop cluster, right?

00:39:43.220 --> 00:39:45.590
What did we do with that?

00:39:45.590 --> 00:39:50.060
So at the end of Q1 this
year, we held a big gathering

00:39:50.060 --> 00:39:53.560
in our Stockholm office, in
the cafeteria on the top floor.

00:39:53.560 --> 00:39:56.470
And we had one of the
first engineers that

00:39:56.470 --> 00:40:00.820
ran a Hadoop job at Spotify
press a big red button shutting

00:40:00.820 --> 00:40:05.320
down the power in our London
data center that was our Hadoop

00:40:05.320 --> 00:40:06.790
cluster.

00:40:06.790 --> 00:40:08.580
And the crowd sat
there and watched

00:40:08.580 --> 00:40:11.500
a live feed of our data center.

00:40:11.500 --> 00:40:14.200
And the first thing
they noticed is all

00:40:14.200 --> 00:40:16.660
this whirring and buzzing
of fans in the data center

00:40:16.660 --> 00:40:18.490
immediately went silent.

00:40:18.490 --> 00:40:19.750
And it was crazy.

00:40:19.750 --> 00:40:22.360
And then one by one, you'd
see all these blinking lights

00:40:22.360 --> 00:40:25.480
stop to blink, and
shut down, and go dark.

00:40:25.480 --> 00:40:28.890
And that really signaled
the end of our life

00:40:28.890 --> 00:40:32.740
as on-premise Hadoop user and
signaled the start of our life

00:40:32.740 --> 00:40:35.860
as fully native on GCP.

00:40:35.860 --> 00:40:39.820
And to complete that
day's celebration,

00:40:39.820 --> 00:40:44.460
we had an "Office Space"-style
dismantling of one of our name

00:40:44.460 --> 00:40:46.830
nodes that was used early on.

00:40:46.830 --> 00:40:50.080
And as you could see there,
really just let the steam out

00:40:50.080 --> 00:40:53.340
and signaled that transition.

00:40:53.340 --> 00:40:57.480
So that concludes the migration.

00:40:57.480 --> 00:40:59.810
We want to highlight-- and
bring everybody on stage--

00:40:59.810 --> 00:41:02.150
and highlight a few of
the most important lessons

00:41:02.150 --> 00:41:04.108
that we learned
throughout the process.

00:41:04.108 --> 00:41:06.548
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:11.711 --> 00:41:12.460
MAX CHARAS: Great.

00:41:12.460 --> 00:41:15.050
So the first lesson is prepare.

00:41:15.050 --> 00:41:16.890
And I think it's
important to state

00:41:16.890 --> 00:41:21.556
here that we prepared
for probably two years

00:41:21.556 --> 00:41:23.055
before actually
doing the migration.

00:41:23.055 --> 00:41:26.160
And each migration
took roughly a year.

00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:29.010
So we spent a lot
of time preparing.

00:41:29.010 --> 00:41:32.340
Now what we really did was we
tried, as you can see here,

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:38.464
a minimal use case that showed
the benefits of moving to GCP.

00:41:38.464 --> 00:41:40.380
But when we say minimal
here, its important to

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:42.600
say that it wasn't
a small thing.

00:41:42.600 --> 00:41:45.510
It had to have been an important
feature for the entire business

00:41:45.510 --> 00:41:50.450
to show the true
value of moving over.

00:41:50.450 --> 00:41:53.220
For each of these
minimal use cases,

00:41:53.220 --> 00:41:56.070
we refined the model
for moving over.

00:41:56.070 --> 00:42:00.830
So over time, it became
better and better.

00:42:00.830 --> 00:42:02.690
One thing that's also
important to note

00:42:02.690 --> 00:42:05.600
is that your early adopters,
if you decide to do this,

00:42:05.600 --> 00:42:06.900
are super important.

00:42:06.900 --> 00:42:09.560
Because they will be
your developer advocates

00:42:09.560 --> 00:42:14.740
later on in the organization
as your migration progresses.

00:42:14.740 --> 00:42:15.250
Great.

00:42:15.250 --> 00:42:18.900
That was the first
lesson, prepare.

00:42:18.900 --> 00:42:21.880
RAMON VAN ALTEREN: I'll
take the second lesson.

00:42:21.880 --> 00:42:23.850
The second lesson is focus.

00:42:23.850 --> 00:42:28.740
It is truly amazing what you
can do with a team of engineers

00:42:28.740 --> 00:42:31.380
if you focus on a single thing.

00:42:31.380 --> 00:42:34.380
We literally had sprints that
lasted a week where we moved

00:42:34.380 --> 00:42:37.290
more than 50 to 70 services.

00:42:37.290 --> 00:42:38.820
It is amazing.

00:42:38.820 --> 00:42:40.140
Do that.

00:42:40.140 --> 00:42:41.490
It helps a lot.

00:42:41.490 --> 00:42:43.470
It will also help your
business stakeholders.

00:42:43.470 --> 00:42:46.239
They'll be a lot happier that
there's a short period of time

00:42:46.239 --> 00:42:48.030
that there's no product
development instead

00:42:48.030 --> 00:42:50.170
of an extended period of time.

00:42:50.170 --> 00:42:52.290
And if you try doing
migrating while doing

00:42:52.290 --> 00:42:55.440
all the other priorities that
you have within the business,

00:42:55.440 --> 00:42:59.130
your migration speed
will slow to a crawl.

00:42:59.130 --> 00:43:02.511
And that will make this a
far more difficult process

00:43:02.511 --> 00:43:03.260
than it has to be.

00:43:08.650 --> 00:43:10.390
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
The third lesson

00:43:10.390 --> 00:43:13.060
is creating a migration team.

00:43:13.060 --> 00:43:16.030
Both of the two different
paths, the services and data

00:43:16.030 --> 00:43:18.490
migration, used a
migration team to help

00:43:18.490 --> 00:43:22.810
enable the teams actually
doing the migrating

00:43:22.810 --> 00:43:24.340
and accelerate that process.

00:43:24.340 --> 00:43:26.920
And the way we looked at
it was to go into teams

00:43:26.920 --> 00:43:29.622
and act as guardrails for them.

00:43:29.622 --> 00:43:32.080
The teams that maintain the
services, know the code better,

00:43:32.080 --> 00:43:33.720
know the services
better than we do.

00:43:33.720 --> 00:43:35.094
But we came in
and said, you know

00:43:35.094 --> 00:43:36.940
what, we're here to
make sure that you

00:43:36.940 --> 00:43:38.590
know what you need to know.

00:43:38.590 --> 00:43:41.980
We can pass on past
experience, past learnings,

00:43:41.980 --> 00:43:43.850
and make this as
efficient as possible.

00:43:43.850 --> 00:43:45.970
We're here to be
resources that you need,

00:43:45.970 --> 00:43:48.389
whether you need to
talk about new libraries

00:43:48.389 --> 00:43:49.930
that you're doing
for data migration,

00:43:49.930 --> 00:43:52.510
or maybe talk to
somebody at Google

00:43:52.510 --> 00:43:54.760
because you're curious about
how something else works.

00:43:54.760 --> 00:43:58.900
And what was great
about that is that we

00:43:58.900 --> 00:44:01.930
got to carry the experience
from each migration

00:44:01.930 --> 00:44:03.640
from team to team to
team, so you're not

00:44:03.640 --> 00:44:06.820
doing, say, five teams
migrating in isolation.

00:44:06.820 --> 00:44:09.822
Team one and team four run
across the same roadblock

00:44:09.822 --> 00:44:12.280
and spend the same amount of
time solving the same problem.

00:44:12.280 --> 00:44:14.825
Well, what we could do
is take that experience

00:44:14.825 --> 00:44:15.700
and carry it forward.

00:44:15.700 --> 00:44:16.533
Carry it throughout.

00:44:16.533 --> 00:44:18.730
And so each sequential
migration actually

00:44:18.730 --> 00:44:20.170
improves on the
previous, in terms

00:44:20.170 --> 00:44:21.461
of what we could carry through.

00:44:21.461 --> 00:44:23.020
And so that was
very, very helpful

00:44:23.020 --> 00:44:24.460
on both sides of
those migrations

00:44:24.460 --> 00:44:26.085
to be able to move
those teams forward.

00:44:27.854 --> 00:44:30.270
JOSH BAER: And the last lesson
that we wanted to highlight

00:44:30.270 --> 00:44:33.480
was to get out of hybrid
as fast as you can.

00:44:33.480 --> 00:44:36.060
So in the data migration,
as we mentioned,

00:44:36.060 --> 00:44:39.120
all these copy jobs back
and forth to clusters

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:43.110
was both expensive, in
terms of network bandwidth,

00:44:43.110 --> 00:44:45.091
but also complex
in terms of people

00:44:45.091 --> 00:44:47.340
had to figure out where the
jobs were actually running

00:44:47.340 --> 00:44:49.380
and where the dependencies were.

00:44:49.380 --> 00:44:51.480
It was also a problem
in the services,

00:44:51.480 --> 00:44:53.239
as Ramon highlighted early on.

00:44:53.239 --> 00:44:54.780
So when you were in
an incident mode,

00:44:54.780 --> 00:44:56.730
you had to figure out
where is the problem?

00:44:56.730 --> 00:44:58.680
Is it in the on-premise tooling?

00:44:58.680 --> 00:45:00.510
Is it in the GCP tooling?

00:45:00.510 --> 00:45:04.590
Or maybe is it in
the glue in between?

00:45:04.590 --> 00:45:05.560
Yeah.

00:45:05.560 --> 00:45:07.570
And this also, as
Ramon mentioned,

00:45:07.570 --> 00:45:10.650
adds cognitive overhead
for your teams.

00:45:10.650 --> 00:45:12.674
And when you want to
focus on the world

00:45:12.674 --> 00:45:14.590
just purely on the cloud,
that's not something

00:45:14.590 --> 00:45:15.220
you want to think of.

00:45:15.220 --> 00:45:17.219
So if you move to the
cloud as fast as possible,

00:45:17.219 --> 00:45:20.260
you could just focus
on being cloud native.

00:45:20.260 --> 00:45:22.740
So that concludes
the presentation.

00:45:22.740 --> 00:45:24.660
We'll now take
questions, I think,

00:45:24.660 --> 00:45:26.520
using the mics on the side.

00:45:26.520 --> 00:45:27.620
Thank you all.

00:45:27.620 --> 00:45:29.420
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:29.420 --> 00:45:32.170
[LOGO MUSIC PLAYING]

